		{
			"id": "110544652404000101",
			"parentId": "110544634846455492",
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@sil/110544652346795225",
			"author": {
				"name": "Stuart Langridge",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@sil",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/302/147/382/032/479/original/ab95a50a2e58e997.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-06-14T21:18:52.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> there is no single thing that can improve your computing experience more than filling every single atom of space with RAM. I was mocked for putting loads in and I have been quietly happy ever since. Buy more memory. My machine is an i5 from a decade ago and I’m hardly ever CPU-bound. It’s always memory pressure. People will sneer and say ours more complicated than that, and I’m sure they’re right, but… drown your OS in memory and it makes it happy.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
